course: dictionary
lesson: 10311
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The word town can mean a place, the centre of a place, or all the people who live there. To find the right meaning, read the words around it, which is its context. «Light pollution hides the Milky Way from most people who live in towns.» Here towns are places where people live and work.
explain: Read this: «Please go into town with me to buy shoes.» Going into town means going to the centre of a place, where the shops are. The word shoes is a clue, because shops sell shoes.
explain: Town can also mean all the people of a place. «Our town voted for a new park» means the people voted, not the buildings. Ask yourself: does the meaning fit a place, the centre, or the people? Then swap your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
fact: A town is a place where many people live and work, bigger than a village and smaller than a city.
fact: «Town» can mean the centre of a place with the shops, as in «go into town».
fact: The people of a town together are also called the town.
fact: «Kyiv» was clearly an important «town» by the 900s, long before Moscow is first mentioned in 1147.
exercise: «Light pollution hides the Milky Way from most people who live in towns.» What does town mean here? | *A place where many people live and work | A small farm far from others | The people who vote in a council | The centre with the shops
exercise: «Please go into town with me to buy shoes.» What does go into town mean? | Sleep in a tent | *Go to the centre of a place with the shops | Climb up a hill | Leave the place for a holiday
exercise: «Our town voted for a new park.» What does town mean here? | *The people of the place, all together | A large building with a clock tower | A kind of weather | A map of roads
test: A maths problem says: a bus travels 12 km from one town to another. What does town mean here? | A long, straight road | The centre of a place with the shops | The people who live in a town | *A place where many people live and work
test: «Medieval towns had public bathhouses, where people washed every day.» Which meaning of town fits this sentence? | A type of boat | *A place where many people lived and worked | The centre with the shops | A group of friends
exam: «Everyone in the town came out to watch the parade.» Which meaning of town is used here? | *The people of the place, all together | The centre with the shops | A place with lots of tall buildings | A village smaller than a town
